WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:02.980
Amelia Earhart
In 1937 Amelia Earhart was one of the most

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:04.950
famous women on the planet.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.070
For the last decade she had been upending
stereotypes, smashing records and establishing

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.599
herself as an international role model.

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:17.270
As she set off for her greatest adventure,
around the world excursion, the whole world

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:18.400
was watching.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.550
Then, suddenly, she was gone – disappeared.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:28.029
In this week’s Biographics we delve into
the marvellous and mysterious life and death

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:33.900
of Amelia Earhart.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:42.910
Beginnings
Amelia Earhart was born on July 24th, 1897

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.490
in Atchison, Kansas.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:49.650
She was the older of two daughters to Edwin
and Amy, with sister Muriel being two years

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:50.650
younger.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:55.480
Edwin Stanton Earhart was a talented lawyer
who worked for the railways, but his career

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.480
was blighted by an addiction to alcohol.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.890
Amelia was named after her maternal grandmother
Millie Otis.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.900
Millie and Amelia’s grandfather, Alfred,
actually took the girl in and she lived with

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.390
them during the school year in Atchison from
the ages of 3 to 11.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.850
During the holidays she went to live with
her parents in Kansas City.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:18.370
Amelia attended a private college preparatory
school until she was ready for high school.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:23.750
Becoming an avid reader, young Amelia spent
many long hours devouring books of every sort.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.580
Even though she spent most of the year away
from him, she adored his witty and well educated

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:29.580
and imaginative father.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.830
Edwin would entertain the girls and their
friends with Western thrillers complete with

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.390
bandits, Indians, cowboys and other made up
characters.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.200
Amelia marvelled over his command of the English
language.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.880
On one occasion he addressed her in a letter
as ‘Dear Parallelepipedon,’ and she rushed

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.600
to find a dictionary to discover the new word’s
meaning.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.710
Despite his brilliance as a wordsmith, Edwin
was not so lucky when it came to financial

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:50.710
matters.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.670
As a railroad lawyer he never really applied
himself, preferring rather to spend his time

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:57.350
coming up with inventions that would make
his fortune.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:02.170
In 1903 he designed a device to hold signal
flags on the ends of trains.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:06.490
Full of enthusiasm he travelled to Washington
DC to file his plans with the patent office.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:10.840
After two frustrating days, he wrote back
to Amelia’s mother that ‘a man from Colorado

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.280
had filed a patent on an identical holder
two years ago . . . this news is a terrible

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.240
blow because I had been counting on receiving
several hundred dollars from the railroad

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.430
for my flag holder.’

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.959
Worse still the money that Edwin had been
counting on was earmarked to pay the family’s

00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:25.980
property taxes.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.220
In order to pay the tax bill, he had to sell
a collection of valuable law books that he

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.849
had been given by his father-in-law, Judge
Otis.

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:34.940
The judge was furious that his son-in-law
had done these things.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:38.950
A year later when Edwin took his wife and
two girls for a week-long trip to the World’s

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:43.530
Fair in St. Louis, the Judge considered the
trip an extravagant waste of money and further

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.170
proof of Edwin’s spendthrift habits.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.480
Despite her grandfather’s reservations,
Amelia loved the trip, as she enthusiastically

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.980
rushed with her sister around the fifteen
hundred buildings and twelve palaces – all

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.040
lit by the still relatively new phenomenon
of electricity.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.270
In her autobiography For the Fun of it, published
in 1932, Amelia pin-pointed three important

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:08.870
threads that attracted her to aviation: an
interest in mechanics, the many railroad trips

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:13.330
she took with her father and her love of experimentation
with sport and games.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:18.060
As a railroad claims agent, Edwin was able
to take his family on trips all over the country.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.620
Her travels developed in Amelia a deep-seated
desire to see more and more of the world at

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:24.620
large.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:28.220
When she wasn’t travelling, Amelia spent
hours sitting with her cousins, Lucy and Kathryn

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:33.470
Challis, looking over maps of faraway places
and dreaming of overseas excursions.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:38.290
From the beginning, the future aviatrix was
a daredevil who acted as if she was immune

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:39.319
to danger.

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:44.239
She was attracted to every type of sport,
but she chafed under the societal stereotypes

00:03:44.239 --> 00:03:47.610
of the day, which limited girls to reading,
sewing and cooking.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.270
She was lucky in that she had parents who
encouraged her tastes for boy’s pursuits

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:55.860
and allowed her and her sister to dress in
dark blue flannel bloomers gathered up at

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.330
the knees for play, rather than the customary
dresses and pinafores.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:04.849
At school, Amelia, who was known as ‘Millie’,
earned good grades, but seemed to be too impatient

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:07.830
to worry about the details of her academic
work.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:12.040
She would quite easily solve math problems
in her head but couldn’t be bothered writing

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.050
down her working out.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:17.479
This lack of attention to detail was a trait
that stayed with Amelia into adulthood.

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:21.870
It was at the Iowa State Fair in 1907 that
Amelia saw her first aeroplane.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:26.059
She recalled it as a thing of rust and wire
that didn’t interest her at all.

00:04:26.059 --> 00:04:27.059
She wasn’t alone.

00:04:27.059 --> 00:04:31.389
At the time neither automobiles nor airplanes
were predicted to become functional means

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:32.800
of transportation.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.600
In 1908, Amelia stopped living with her grandparents
in Atchison and moved with her parents to

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:38.689
Des Moines, Iowa.

00:04:38.689 --> 00:04:40.319
There she entered public school.

00:04:40.319 --> 00:04:43.039
She did well academically, despite problems
at home.

00:04:43.039 --> 00:04:47.479
Her parents argued constantly, mainly over
money, and the constant quarrelling drove

00:04:47.479 --> 00:04:49.529
her beloved father to the bottle.

00:04:49.529 --> 00:04:53.840
This was at a time when states across America
were increasingly becoming ‘dry.’

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.419
Public displays of drunkenness were a source
of great humiliation.

00:04:57.419 --> 00:05:02.080
As a result, the pitiful public inebriation
of Edwin Stanton Earhart soon became the talk

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.250
of the town.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:10.009
Hardship
The heavy drinking eventually led to Edwin

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:11.710
losing his job at the railway.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:16.139
For over a year he remained out of work, finally
securing a menial clerking job.

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:18.550
It required that the family move to St. Paul.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:19.550
Minnesota.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:24.020
The departure from Iowa was a depressing one
for the girls, but especially for their long-suffering

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:25.020
mother.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.939
As they boarded the railroad car to St. Paul,
she shed tears of frustration.

00:05:28.939 --> 00:05:32.210
Life was tough in St. Paul, with the family
barely making ends meet.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.949
Edwin’s drinking was still not under control.

00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:38.699
On one occasion, Amelia found a bottle of
whiskey in her father’s suitcase and proceeded

00:05:38.699 --> 00:05:40.159
to empty it into the kitchen sink.

00:05:40.159 --> 00:05:43.740
An infuriated Edwin tried to slap the girl,
but Amy stopped him.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.889
The constant moves her family undertook during
her teen years left Amelia feeling rootless

00:05:48.889 --> 00:05:50.050
and insecure.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:55.430
But, rather than discussing her problems,
she instead focused on her ambitions.

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.589
The family only stayed in St. Paul for a year.

00:05:57.589 --> 00:06:01.789
With a job awaiting Edwin in the claims office
of the Burlington Railway, they relocated

00:06:01.789 --> 00:06:03.249
to Springfield, Missouri.

00:06:03.249 --> 00:06:05.490
The move was a disaster.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:09.349
After a seven-hour train journey the family
discovered that the job had already been taken.

00:06:09.349 --> 00:06:11.719
For Amy this was the final straw.

00:06:11.719 --> 00:06:16.360
She demanded a trial separation and took the
two girls to Chicago to stay with old family

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.360
friends.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.819
In Chicago, Amelia shouldered responsibility
for her mother and sister as head of the household.

00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:24.789
Her behaviour and appearance changed dramatically.

00:06:24.789 --> 00:06:28.599
Although she attended Hyde Park, the best
public high school in Chicago, she did not

00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:32.349
participate in any of the numerous clubs the
school offered, nor did she play in any of

00:06:32.349 --> 00:06:34.020
the sports teams she so loved.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:38.559
She now referred to herself as A.E. and isolated
herself from her fellow students.

00:06:38.559 --> 00:06:41.360
She refused to attend the senior banquet and
graduation ceremony.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.759
Her senior yearbook photo caption read, ‘The
girl in brown who walks alone.’

00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:49.629
By the fall of 1915, things had improved in
the Earhart marriage.

00:06:49.629 --> 00:06:53.610
Edwin had stopped drinking and the family
had reunited in Kansas City.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:58.050
For the next year, Amelia did not attend school,
but spent her time acting as a buffer between

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.060
her parents and acting to preserve the marriage.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.740
The family fortunes took an immediate upturn
when Amie received an inheritance.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.629
She used some of the funds to send Amelia
to the Ogontz finishing school on the outskirts

00:07:08.629 --> 00:07:09.629
of Philadelphia.

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:13.460
It was there that Amelia polished her social
graces, which would allow her to move with

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:17.789
ease and poise among all segments of society
after she became famous.

00:07:17.789 --> 00:07:23.360
At nineteen, she was the oldest of Ogontz
students when she entered in the fall of 1916.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.399
While she was at Ogontz, Amelia’s feminist
proclivities came to the fore.

00:07:26.399 --> 00:07:30.619
She kept a newspaper clipping file of accomplished
women, many of them firsts in their field.

00:07:30.619 --> 00:07:32.770
But she was no suffragette.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:36.770
Next to one clipping she wrote . . . ‘Women
will gain economic justice by proving themselves

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:41.289
in all lines of endeavor, not by having laws
passed for them.’

00:07:41.289 --> 00:07:44.129
Over the first semester Christmas break, Amelia
returned home.

00:07:44.129 --> 00:07:47.709
While there she witnessed the ravages of the
First World War; returned soldiers on the

00:07:47.709 --> 00:07:51.830
street with missing limbs, blinded and otherwise
maimed.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:54.789
She returned to Ogontz but felt conflicted.

00:07:54.789 --> 00:07:58.819
She wrote to her mother, ‘I can’t bear
the thought of going back to school and being

00:07:58.819 --> 00:08:00.229
so useless.’

00:08:00.229 --> 00:08:05.219
Amelia decided to quit the school and enrolled
in a course to join a Voluntary Aid Detachment,

00:08:05.219 --> 00:08:10.710
a group of civilians who served as assistant
nurses, ambulance drivers and nurses.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:15.499
After several months of training, Sister Amelia,
as she was known, was posted to Spadinia Military

00:08:15.499 --> 00:08:18.949
Hospital, on the campus of the University
of Toronto.

00:08:18.949 --> 00:08:23.330
Working there until just before the armistice
in 1918, she cared for soldiers who had been

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:27.639
poisoned by gas and otherwise injured in the
trenches of the Western Front.

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:31.610
She also looked after pilots from the Royal
Flying Corps Canada who had crashed in Europe.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:36.280
It was while tending to these injured pilots
that Amelia became fascinated with the idea

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:37.280
of flying.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:40.550
The men would trade stories about their exploits
high above the ground.

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:45.050
Nearby was the Royal Flying Corps base and
the skies were filled with hundreds of planes

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:46.340
buzzing around.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:50.080
Civilians were not allowed to fly in the planes
but on her days off, Amelia spent time with

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.660
the pilots attended aerial exhibitions.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:57.350
The hospital job was intensely demanding,
with Amelia often working 12-hour shifts.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:01.620
She becam so worn down that, in the summer
of 1918, she came down with influenza and

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.450
pneumonia and developed painful sinusitis.

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:09.030
As a result, she suffered from chronic sinus
infections throughout the rest of her life.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:13.360
After a period of convalescence with her mother,
Amelia enrolled at Columbia University studying

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.800
zoology, biology and chemistry.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:17.950
She rented a modest apartment in New York
City.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.360
At the end of the spring term, she quit her
studies.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:24.090
She would later claim that she had simply
decided against a medical career.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:29.180
The reality was that she lacked the temperament
to wade through years of tertiary study.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:33.500
Spending the next year with her parents in
Los Angeles, Amelia put her focus on helping

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.300
them keep their marriage intact.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:39.720
Over the course of that year, the now 22-year
old Amelia would take her first paying job,

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:43.690
drive her first car and fly her first lane.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:51.450
First Flight
Los Angeles in 1920 was a haven for pilots.

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:57.280
It was full of barnstormers, pilots who stunted
in air circuses and gave rides to often terrified

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:58.280
passengers.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:03.150
In December, 1920 Amelia and her father went
to an air rodeo in Long Beach, where pilots

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:06.890
performed spins, loops and dives amid a crowd
of spectators.

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:11.550
The next day Edwin paid ten dollars to give
his daughter her first passenger flight, a

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:13.540
ten-minute ride over the Hollywood Hills.

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:19.800
The moment the plane left the ground she knew
that she herself had to fly.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:21.710
She had found her calling.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:25.110
Without informing her parents, Amelia signed
up for flying lessons, with the expectation

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:27.240
that Edwin would cover the $500 fee.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:32.030
But her father had no interest in his daughter’s
new-found obsession and she was forced to

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:34.940
find a job as a telephone clerk in order to
fund the lessons.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:39.580
She chose to be instructed by a female pilot,
Anita ‘Neta’ Snook.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:42.840
After ten hours of lessons, Amelia went solo
in 1921.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:47.410
The exhilarating experience encouraged her
on to achieving her full pilot’s licence.

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:52.010
Over the next few months, as she progressed
in her lessons, Amelia became an airfield

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:53.010
junkie.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:57.130
She cropped her long hair and began wearing
breeches, boots and an oil stained leather

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:58.130
jacket.

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:00.930
Despite complaining about the high cost of
lessons, she managed to scrape together the

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:06.450
money needed to buy her first plane (thanks
largely to her mother’s inheritance), an

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.600
Airster with a 28-foot wingspan.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:11.330
It was just the second ever Airster ever built.

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.340
She named the plane Canary.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:17.080
During these early years, Amelia didn’t
consider herself a good enough flyer to appear

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:18.370
in public flying events.

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:22.580
Burt her humility didn’t preclude a few
appearances at air shows and two attempts

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:24.740
at record setting in the LA area.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:28.480
The press was also mentioning her as one of
the few women pilots in the area.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.221
By 1923 she had turned into a confident pilot,
though she was not a natural flyer and had

00:11:33.221 --> 00:11:35.260
struggled throughout her instruction.

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:39.660
Many men, and even some women, were better
pilots than Amelia – but her persistence

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:41.660
and bravery set her apart.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:45.090
In 1924, Amelia’s parents’ marriage finally
ended in divorce.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:50.450
A devastated Amelia saw no reason to stay
in California and she relocated to Boston,

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:52.130
along with her mother and sister.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:56.791
The following year she re-enrolled at Columbia,
switching to a major in engineering, but when

00:11:56.791 --> 00:12:00.000
she scored a C- in Algebra she once again
quit her studies.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.930
She then applied for admittance to MIT but
was turned down.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:10.520
A World Record
Over the next few years, Amelia worked at

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:12.060
a number of menial jobs.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:15.780
Eventually she ended up as a social worker
at a settlement house in Boston.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:20.980
In 1928, her 3-year hiatus from flying ended
when she agreed to take a set as a passenger

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:23.390
and log keeper on a flight from New York to
London.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:27.920
The plane was a Fokker F.VII- 3-meter floatplane
piloted by Wilmer Stultz.

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:32.370
The floatplane took off on the morning of
June 17and arrived at Bury Point in South

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:35.040
Wales nearly 21 hours later.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:36.980
Despite her role being a minimal one.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:41.480
Amelia was now in the books as the first woman
to fly the North Atlantic.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:45.920
Returning to the USA, Amelia developed a close
relationship with George Putnam, a New York

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:47.590
businessman and former publisher.

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:50.630
They had met when she had interviewed for
a place on the Fokker.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:54.910
Putnam helped her to improve her flying skills
and to embark on a series of lecture tours

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:59.120
intended to promote the aircraft industry
and encourage women to become pilots.

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:04.510
Over the next few months she also wrote her
first autobiography, 20 hr 40 min, about her

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:06.270
flight across the North Atlantic.

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:11.760
On November 22nd, 1929, Amelia set a woman’s
speed record for a single engine monoplane.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.050
The following year she purchased a Lockheed
Vega Executive and, in it, she broke the speed

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:18.500
record for a course of 100 km.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:22.170
Amelia and George Putnam were married on 7th
February, 1931.

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:25.760
Over the next year, she set and broke several
altitude records.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:31.160
On 20th May 1932, she took off from New Foundland
in her Vega, touching down in a field of cows

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:34.850
in Londonderry, Ireland fifteen hours and
thirty minutes later.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:41.030
She had just become the first woman to fly
solo across the North Atlantic.

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:45.720
Fame
With her husband’s flair for publicity she

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.779
quickly became the most famous woman flyer
on the planet.

00:13:48.779 --> 00:13:53.090
Putnam billed his wife as ‘Lady Lindy’,
capitalizing on the massive popularity of

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:54.480
Charles Lindbergh.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.790
Amelia was now a fully-fledged celebrity.

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:00.810
When she made public appearances, she needed
police protection.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:04.630
She branched out by designing a range of clothing
that copied her look.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:09.500
In January, 1935, Amelia became the first
woman to fly from Hawaii to California.

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:14.050
In that year she also wrote her second biography,
The Fun of It, while continuing a hectic schedule

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:17.830
of lecture tours and public appearances.

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:26.790
The Final Challenge
On July 20, 1935, Amelia celebrated her 38th

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:27.790
birthday.

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:30.370
She knew that she couldn’t go on breaking
records forever.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:35.070
Yet she still had one more big one to knock
off – in fact, the biggest of them all;

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:37.880
a flight around the world.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:41.590
To achieve a round the world flight, Amelia
would need a bigger plane.

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:46.820
In early 1936, she purchased a twin-engine
Lockheed 10-E Electra, a plane which was the

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:49.400
product of the finest technology available
at the time.

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:54.250
It was a low wing monoplane of all-metal construction
with constant-speed propellers powered by

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:59.910
two Pratt and Whitney Wasp S3H14 550 horsepower
engines.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:04.210
The interior of the plane was modified to
take extra petrol tanks, giving a theoretical

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:08.650
range of 4,000 miles at an altitude of 4,000
feet.

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:12.910
The plane was also fitted with one of the
very first Sperry automatic pilots in order

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:16.220
to relieve the physical strain of flying on
Amelia.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:20.590
Despite this innovation, it became apparent
that Amelia would need navigational assistance

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:22.600
to fly across the Pacific Ocean.

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:27.410
Her navigational knowledge was rudimentary
and so Irish born navigator Fred Noonan was

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:29.840
recruited for the world record attempt.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:34.370
Noonan was keen to be involved but there was
one aspect of the navigational set up that

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:35.400
he wasn’t happy with.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:39.900
The only way the navigator could reach the
pilot was by crawling along a catwalk over

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:41.550
the intervening fuel tanks.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:45.339
A sample system was devised whereby written
messages could be clipped to the line of a

00:15:45.339 --> 00:15:48.029
bamboo fishing pole and passed between them.

00:15:48.029 --> 00:15:57.660
But this inadequate method impaired the crucial
element of teamwork.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:15:59.080
The Final Flight
The planned route for the round the world

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:04.140
flight began in Oakland, California with the
first landing place at Honolulu, Hawaii.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:08.680
From there, it continued in a westerly direction,
taking advantage of prevailing winds.

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:13.100
In order to maximise publicity, Putman required
his wife to keep a detailed diary and post

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:14.760
them to him from each stop.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:18.560
He would edit them so that he could rush out
a book as soon she had achieved the record.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:23.810
The first leg of the round the world flight
took off from Oakland at 1630 on 17th March,

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:24.810
1937.

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:29.370
The flight to Honolulu was smooth and uneventful,
taking 15 hours and 47 minutes.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:35.520
The next stop was to be the tiny American
territory of Howland Island, 1,800 miles away.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:39.010
Immediately things went wrong – the plane
swung to the right just after take-off, then

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:41.600
ground looping as the under carriage collapsed.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:44.960
Fortunately, Amelia managed to land without
any injury or fire.

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:49.310
But the world record attempt had to be put
on hold as the plane was crated and sent back

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:50.960
to Lockheed for rebuilding.

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:53.610
The rebuilding was completed on May 17.

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:57.529
Efforts were made to reduce the weight of
the plane, one of which undoubtedly contributed

00:16:57.529 --> 00:16:59.240
to the tragedy to come.

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:04.360
The trailing aerial which allowed them to
request bearings on 500 kHz from ground stations

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:05.360
was removed.

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:10.139
Three days after the rebuilt Elektra was delivered
to them, Amelia and Noonan took off from Oakland

00:17:10.139 --> 00:17:11.220
for the second time.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:15.829
Because if seasonal changes, it was decided
that this new attempt would be done in the

00:17:15.829 --> 00:17:17.059
reverse direction.

00:17:17.059 --> 00:17:21.870
The first leg was to Miami and then on to
Puerto Rico, Venezuela and Dutch Guinea.

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:23.629
In early June they flew to Fortaleza in Brazil.

00:17:23.629 --> 00:17:27.970
The following days were sent servicing the
engines and checking the instruments.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:33.759
The 1,900-mile flight across the south Atlantic
was made on 7th June, landing in Dakar, Senegal.

00:17:33.759 --> 00:17:36.389
The journey across Africa lasted four days.

00:17:36.389 --> 00:17:41.059
It was followed by the longest leg of 1950
km to Karachi, India.

00:17:41.059 --> 00:17:45.809
There the plane was serviced by Imperial Airways
along with members of the British RAF.

00:17:45.809 --> 00:17:50.280
On June 17th, they flew on to Dum-Dum near
Calcutta, landing in the middle of a torrential

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:51.280
rainstorm.

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:53.619
After refuelling at Bangkok, they flew on
to Singapore.

00:17:53.619 --> 00:17:55.720
From there it was on to Bandoeng in Java.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:58.369
Here the Electra developed some kind of instrument
fault.

00:17:58.369 --> 00:18:02.470
On the flight to Surabaya in East Java the
problem recurred, forcing them back to Bandoeng

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:04.539
where there were repair facilities.

00:18:04.539 --> 00:18:07.559
On June 29th, they flew to Lae in New Guinea.

00:18:07.559 --> 00:18:11.850
The 1,200-mile flight was completed in 7 hours
and 43 minutes.

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:14.820
So far, Amelia and Noon had travelled 22,000
miles.

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:19.490
They must have been mightily exhausted; yet
the greatest challenge still lay before them

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:24.519
– the crossing of the Pacific.

00:18:24.519 --> 00:18:30.150
Disappeared
The next objective was Howland Island, some

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:33.590
2,566 miles away.

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:36.960
The two spent June 30th in preparation for
the mammoth flight.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:40.830
Several witnesses later reported that Noonan
was drinking heavily on this day and was still

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:44.290
doing so into the evening as Amelia attended
a dinner party.

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:46.559
They took off at 10am on July 2nd.

00:18:46.559 --> 00:18:50.679
Eyewitnesses reported that the Electra lifted
off sluggishly, sank downwards toward the

00:18:50.679 --> 00:18:54.159
sea and then began a very slow climb to the
east.

00:18:54.159 --> 00:18:55.840
No one has ever seen them since.

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:59.050
The last known position of the Electra was
near the Nukumanu Islands.

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:03.539
The last radio message from Amelia was . . .
We must be on you, but gas is running low.

00:19:03.539 --> 00:19:05.570
Have been unable to reach you by radio.

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:10.210
We are flying at 1,000 feet.

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:12.919
What Happened?

00:19:12.919 --> 00:19:17.340
The official search effort to find the missing
plane and its famous occupant lasted until

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.340
July 19th 1937.

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:24.720
The air and sea search by the US Navy and
Coast Guard was the most expensive in US history.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:29.919
Yet no physical evidence of the plane or the
remains of Amelia and Noonan were found.

00:19:29.919 --> 00:19:34.330
Over the decades the speculation over what
happened to Amelia Earhart has intensified.

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:38.870
In 2017, a photo emerged that purported to
show a back view of Amelia sitting on a pier

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:39.890
in the Marshall Islands.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:44.470
However, this photo was debunked when it was
proven that it was actually taken in 1935,

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.429
two years before Amelia disappeared.

00:19:46.429 --> 00:19:51.409
Then, in March, 2018 an anthropology professor
at the University of Tennessee named Richard

00:19:51.409 --> 00:19:57.570
Jantz argued that bones found on the island
of Nikumaroro in 1940 were 99% likely to be

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:59.159
those of Amelia Earhart.

00:19:59.159 --> 00:20:02.549
The bones were analysed in 1941 and determined
to be those of a male.

00:20:02.549 --> 00:20:05.840
But Jantz believes that that analysis was
deeply flawed.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:08.500
Yet other scientists uphold the original findings.

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:13.669
While we may never know what really happened
to Amelia, it appears quite certain that the

00:20:13.669 --> 00:20:35.070
tantalizing mystery of her disappearance will
keep her name in the headlines for many years

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:52.250
to come.

